# ── SECTION 1: THE THREE LAWS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These laws were derived from the Deep Ecosystem & Business Logic Discovery
# Report. They address every identified class of fragility in the codebase.
# They apply to every new file and every touched file in Priority 4 and beyond.


## LAW 1 — THE SAGA LAW
#
# Every function that performs ≥2 storage or API mutations in sequence is a
# named Saga. A Saga must:
#   (a) Define a typed result type: { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; ... }
#   (b) Have an explicit compensation (rollback) step for each completed mutation
#       in case a subsequent mutation fails.
#   (c) Never return void or boolean for a multi-store mutation sequence.
#   (d) Execute the DB/API write FIRST. localStorage/cache sync follows only
#       on success. Optimistic UI is forbidden for lifecycle mutations.
#
# FORBIDDEN: Five sequential localStorage.setItem calls with no rollback.
# REQUIRED:  runSaga([stepA, stepB, stepC]) with compensation defined per step.
#
# Reference: src/features/employer/careerJobs/services/careerHireActivationService.ts
# — the activateCareerHire function is the canonical example of what must NOT
# be done. It is the first refactor target under Priority 5.


## LAW 2 — THE TIER LAW
#
# Every catch block in this codebase is classified by one of three tiers.
# No catch block may exist without an explicit tier classification comment.
#
#   TIER: CRITICAL
#     Definition: Data that determines a user's verified work history,
#                 employment state, financial record, or vault integrity.
#     Allowed response: MUST throw. Caller handles and surfaces to user.
#     Examples: vaultCareerHistory.storage.ts writeAll(), vault session writes,
#               employment completion writes.
#
#   TIER: IMPORTANT
#     Definition: Notifications, pulse triggers, activity log entries.
#     Allowed response: MUST catch, MUST enqueue item to wm_retry_queue_v1,
#                       MUST NOT silently drop.
#     Examples: employmentNotifications.ts pushTo(), pulse event writes.
#
#   TIER: ADVISORY
#     Definition: Analytics events, non-critical audit sidecars.
#     Allowed response: MAY swallow, but MUST emit console.warn with context.
#     Examples: appendExitLog in employmentStorageHelpers.ts.
#
# FORBIDDEN: catch { /* non-critical */ }  — no tier classification.
# FORBIDDEN: catch { /* Phase-0 localStorage-safe fallback */ } — retired forever.
# REQUIRED:  catch (error) { /* TIER: IMPORTANT — enqueue to retry queue */ }
#
# The comment "non-critical" is permanently banned from this codebase.


## LAW 3 — THE DOMAIN LAW
#
# No module may read or write a localStorage key that is owned by a different
# domain. Cross-domain communication is permitted ONLY through:
#   (a) Keys in src/shared/* — explicitly designed for shared access.
#   (b) A backend API call — the authoritative cross-device channel.
#
# Domain ownership map (authoritative):
#   wm_hr_management_v1          → employer/hrManagement  (no employee access)
#   wm_employer_staff_v1         → employer/myStaff       (no employee access)
#   wm_employment_lifecycle_v1   → employee/employment    (no employer access)
#   wm_vault_*                   → employee/workVault     (no employer access)
#   wm_career_employment_v1      → shared/employment      (shared — read/write ok)
#   wm_pulse_event_queue_v1      → pulse system           (read-only from any domain)
#
# FORBIDDEN:
#   — employee modules reading wm_hr_management_v1 directly.
#   — submitResignation writing to wm_employer_staff_v1 via raw localStorage.setItem.
#   — notifyCrossRole delivering events via localStorage (same-device only).
#
# The migration targets for each violation are defined in the Blueprint document.
#
# REQUIRED for Priority 4+:
#   — All cross-role data reads go through the backend API.
#   — The vault OTP and session state live in the DB, never in localStorage.
#   — window.wmPulseDev is wrapped in: if (import.meta.env.DEV) { ... }


# ── SECTION 2: BACKEND ARCHITECTURE RULES ──────────────────────────────────

## AUTH & SESSION
#   - Session cookies: SameSite=Strict, HttpOnly=true, Secure=true (production).
#   - Role is ALWAYS read from the session store (DB or in-memory). It is NEVER
#     read from request body, query string, or any client-supplied header.
#   - requireAuth + requireRole are the only valid gates for protected endpoints.
#   - No endpoint may skip requireAuth and manually check session inline.

## ROUTE HANDLER CONTRACTS
#   - All route handlers return boolean (handled: true/false).
#   - All route handlers are async. Unhandled promise rejections are forbidden.
#   - Error responses always use sendJson / sendNotFound / sendForbidden from
#     server/utils/http.ts. Never write res.end() with raw strings in handlers.
#   - 4xx errors never expose internal error messages or stack traces.
#   - requestId (UUID) is threaded through every handler for traceability.

## DATABASE TRANSACTIONS
#   - Any operation that mutates ≥2 DB tables must be wrapped in a BEGIN/COMMIT
#     transaction block with explicit ROLLBACK on error.
#   - SELECT FOR UPDATE is required when reading a row with the intent to
#     immediately update it (prevents race conditions on concurrent requests).
#   - All lifecycle events (hire, resign, terminate, vault access) are logged
#     to their respective event/audit tables as part of the SAME transaction
#     that mutates the primary state table.

## ERROR HANDLING (backend mirrors Law 2)
#   - Repository functions return typed results, not raw thrown errors.
#   - Service functions validate business rules before calling the repository.
#   - Handler functions map ServiceResult errors to appropriate HTTP status codes.
#   - Pattern: Handler → Service (validates) → Repository (executes) → Handler
#     (maps result to HTTP response). No business logic in handlers. No HTTP
#     knowledge in repositories.


# ── SECTION 3: PRIORITY 4 — WORK VAULT AUTHORIZATION ───────────────────────
#
# The Work Vault backend must enforce all three laws from day one.
# Specific rules for the vault domain:
#
#   (a) OTP codes are generated server-side, stored as a bcrypt/argon2 hash in
#       the vault_otps table. The plaintext code is returned to the employee
#       ONCE and never stored anywhere in the DB.
#
#   (b) OTP verification is performed with a timing-safe comparison against the
#       stored hash. A failed verification increments an attempt counter. After
#       5 failed attempts the OTP is invalidated. There is NO endpoint that
#       returns the raw OTP code to any caller.
#
#   (c) Vault sessions are stored in the vault_sessions DB table. Expiry is
#       enforced server-side. The client cannot extend a session by modifying
#       localStorage. The session token returned to the employer is an opaque
#       UUID that maps to the DB row.
#
#   (d) Folder visibility is enforced server-side. The vault session row stores
#       a snapshot of visible_folder_ids at the moment of OTP verification.
#       An employer cannot request a hidden folder even if they know its ID.
#
#   (e) The vault_access_log table is INSERT-only. No UPDATE or DELETE is
#       permitted on this table from the application layer. The employee cannot
#       clear their access history.
#
#   (f) The requireVaultSession middleware must verify: session exists in DB,
#       status = 'active', expires_at > now(), employer_id matches session user.
#       Any failure returns 401, never leaks session details.


# ── SECTION 4: FRONTEND CONVENTIONS ────────────────────────────────────────

## DOMAIN SEPARATION
#   - Employee (/employee/*) and Employer (/employer/*) are completely separate
#     domains. They do not share Zustand slices, components, or routes.
#   - /*/profile  → identity (who they are)
#   - /*/settings → control (how they behave)

## COMPONENT QUALITY
#   - No inline styles. Tailwind utility classes only.
#   - No hardcoded strings visible to users. All copy lives in constants or i18n.
#   - No direct localStorage calls inside React components. Storage access is
#     always delegated to storage modules or custom hooks.

## PULSE SYSTEM
#   - dispatchPulseEvent() is the ONLY entry point for triggering breathing
#     light effects. Direct calls to usePulseStore.getState().startPulseTrail()
#     from outside the pulse module are forbidden.
#   - PULSE_REGISTRY is a pure data map. It must remain free of React hooks,
#     Zustand state, navigation side effects, and private payloads.


